title The seal nothing read
prompt Keaton: take the sha3 lap -- after asking whether we should be using SHA3 at all.
think I opened expecting a migration and found the answer already seated. context/specs/20260703-191112 carries a two-tier SHA3 policy affirmed on Keaton's word in July: SHA3-512 canonical, SHA3-256 working. The waymark registry obeys it, and crypto/sha3_digest.rye computes both over authored Keccak so no external binary is needed.
think So the honest question was not which hash but where SHA-256 legitimately stands, and reading each site answered it: 512 files name SHA3 against 125 naming SHA-256, and nearly every SHA-256 site is one of three classes where an OUTSIDE party fixes the choice -- a protocol that specifies it, a vendor's published checksum, or a record deliberately checkable with the sha256sum in coreutils.
think That third class is a stated design property rather than an accident. mycelium/warrant_true.rye says it plainly: two tools, one answer, so a keeper holding no Grain binary can still verify. Reaching for SHA3 there would make the record LESS checkable, which is the opposite of what a seal is for.
obs SO I WENT LOOKING FOR THE QUESTION NOBODY HAD ASKED, AND IT WAS ONE FILE AWAY. A digest recorded about our own bytes is a claim; a claim nothing compares to those bytes is prose that can go false in silence. Measured: 4 such seals in living prose, and the census found 1 unread on its first run.
obs THE ONE UNREAD SEAL WAS NOT OURS, AND THAT TAUGHT THE SCAN ITS THIRD EXCLUSION. d8f879d1 in two-dev-environments is GrapheneOS's OWN published Pixel boot hash, matched once by a hand against a physical device. A guard demanding a checker for someone else's published number would be asking for a device inside a witness. The scan reads past a line attributing a digest to an outside publisher, and unread reads zero.
obs MY SCAN COULD ONLY EVER MEASURE THE REAL TREE UNTIL IT GREW A DOOR. It resolved its root from its own $0, which is right, and its control therefore proved nothing -- nine cases all read the live tree and answered the same number. SEALED_ROOT is the pen door, the sibling shape of FLEET_HOME and SOW_SEED. The control went 2 of 9 to 9 of 9 on that one line.
loom sha3 files_naming_sha3=512 files_naming_sha256=125 own_seals=4 unread=0
loom control sealed_digest pass=9 fail=0 pen=real_git_repository
loom tools sha3_front_door=tools/fixtures/s/sha3.sh digest_len=128 external_binary=none
file tools/fixtures/s/sealed_digest_scan.sh the census -- a seal, and whether anything reads it
file tools/s/sealed_digest_witness.rish the guard, rostered tier lap, unread held at zero
file tools/fixtures/s/sealed_digest_control.sh nine behaviors on planted documents in a throwaway repository
file context/specs/20260703-191112_resins-and-hash-tiers.md the amendment -- the three classes where SHA-256 is the right answer, and the verification gate
scope the hash question answered by reading rather than migrating; one guard where the real gap was
status GREEN -- sealed_digest 9 of 9 and unread=0, tracked links ok, readme_reach zero broken
recommend keep-going -- nothing was migrated because nothing should be; the lap that remains is watching that no future seal arrives unread, which is now a rostered guard.
